\fs96 \cf2 Value creation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The life of a value in Mojo begins when a variable is initialized and continues up until the value is last used, at which point Mojo destroys it. This page describes how every value in Mojo is created, copied, and moved. (The next page describes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 how values are destroyed}}.)\
All data types in Mojo\'97including basic types in the standard library such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/bool/Bool/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Bool}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Int/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Int}}, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string/String/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 String}}\'97are defined as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 structs}}. This means the creation and destruction of any piece of data follows the same lifecycle rules, and you can define your own data types that work exactly the same way.\
Mojo structs don't get any default lifecycle methods, such as a constructor, copy constructor, or move constructor. That means you can define a struct without a constructor, but then you can't instantiate it, and it would be useful only as a sort of namespace for static methods. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 NoInstances\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  state\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 print_hello\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf16 "Hello world!"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Without a constructor, this can't be instantiated, so it has no lifecycle. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 state
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field is also useless because it can't be initialized (Mojo structs do not support default field values\'97you must initialize them in a constructor).\
So the only thing you can do is call the static method:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 NoInstances\cf5 .\cf14 print_hello\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Hello world!\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Constructor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To create an instance of a Mojo type, it needs the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor method. The main responsibility of the constructor is to initialize all fields. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPet\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  age\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now we can create an instance:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  mine \cf5 =\cf14  MyPet\cf5 (\cf16 "Loki"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 An instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can also be read and destroyed, but it currently can't be copied or moved.\
We believe this is a good default starting point, because there are no built-in lifecycle events and no surprise behaviors. You\'97the type author\'97must explicitly decide whether and how the type can be copied or moved, by implementing the copy and move constructors.\
The pattern shown above\'97a constructor that takes an argument for each of the struct's fields and initializes the fields directly from the arguments\'97is called a field-wise constructor. It's a common enough pattern that Mojo includes a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/decorators/fieldwise-init/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 @fieldwise_init}} decorator to synthesize the field-wise constructor. So you can rewrite the previous example like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPet\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 Mojo does not require a destructor to destroy an instance. But in some cases, you may need to define a custom destructor to release resources (for example, if a struct dynamically allocates memory using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb21 \ul UnsafePointer}}). We'll discuss that more in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Death of a value}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb20 The "constructor" name\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 In a Python class, object construction happens across both the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 __new__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 __init__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  methods, so the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 __init__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  method is technically just the initializer for attributes (but it's often still called the constructor). However, in a Mojo struct, there is no 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 __new__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  method, so we prefer to always call 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 __init__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  the constructor.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Overloading the constructor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Like any other function/method, you can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#overloaded-functions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 overload}} the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor to initialize the object with different arguments. For example, you might want a default constructor that sets some default values and takes no arguments, and then additional constructors that accept more arguments.\
Just be aware that, in order to modify any fields, each constructor must declare the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 out
\fs32 \cb1  convention}}. If you want to call one constructor from another, you simply call upon that constructor as you would externally (you don't need to pass 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
For example, here's how you can delegate work from an overloaded constructor:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPet\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 ""\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 0\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self \cf5 =\cf14  MyPet\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Field initialization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Notice in the previous example that, by the end of each constructor, all fields must be initialized. That's the only requirement in the constructor.\
In fact, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor is smart enough to treat the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object as fully initialized even before the constructor is finished, as long as all fields are initialized. For example, this constructor can pass around 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as soon as all fields are initialized:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use\cf5 (\cf14 arg\cf5 :\cf14  MyPet\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPet\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  cond\cf5 :\cf14  Bool\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 if\cf14  cond\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  age\cb1 \
\cb13             use\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Safe to use immediately!\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  age\cb1 \
\cb13         use\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Safe to use immediately!\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Constructors and implicit conversion\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo supports implicit conversion from one type to another. Implicit conversion can happen when one of the following occurs:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You assign a value of one type to a variable with a different type.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You pass a value of one type to a function that requires a different type.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You return a value of one type from a function that specifies a different return type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In all cases, implicit conversion is supported when the target type defines a constructor that meets the following criteria:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Is declared with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @implicit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Has a single required, non-keyword argument of the source type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  a \cf5 =\cf14  Source\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  b\cf5 :\cf14  Target \cf5 =\cf14  a\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo implicitly converts the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Source
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Target
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value if 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Target
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  defines a matching constructor like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Target\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 @implicit\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  s\cf5 :\cf14  Source\cf5 ):\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 With implicit conversion, the assignment above is essentially identical to:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  b \cf5 =\cf14  Target\cf5 (\cf14 a\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In general, types should only support implicit conversions when the conversion is lossless and ideally inexpensive. For example, converting an integer to a floating-point number is usually lossless (except for very large positive and negative integers, where the conversion may be approximate), but converting a floating-point number to an integer is very likely to lose information. So Mojo supports implicit conversion from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but not the reverse.\
The constructor used for implicit conversion can take optional arguments, so the following constructor would also support implicit conversion from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Source
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Target
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Target\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 @implicit\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  s\cf5 :\cf14  Source\cf5 ,\cf14  reverse\cf5 :\cf14  Bool \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 False\cf5 ):\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Implicit conversion can fail if Mojo can't unambiguously match the conversion to a constructor. For example, if the target type has two overloaded constructors that take different types, and each of those types supports an implicit conversion from the source type, the compiler has two equally-valid paths to convert the values:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 A\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 @implicit\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  s\cf5 :\cf14  Source\cf5 ):\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 B\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 @implicit\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  s\cf5 :\cf14  Source\cf5 ):\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 OverloadedTarget\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 @implicit\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  a\cf5 :\cf14  A\cf5 ):\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 @implicit\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  b\cf5 :\cf14  B\cf5 ):\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  t \cf5 =\cf14  OverloadedTarget\cf5 (\cf14 Source\cf5 ())\cf14  \cf6 # Error: ambiguous call to '__init__': each\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13                                    \cf6 # candidate requires 1 implicit conversion\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this case, you can fix the issue by explicitly casting to one of the intermediate types. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  t \cf5 =\cf14  OverloadedTarget\cf5 (\cf14 A\cf5 (\cf14 Source\cf5 ()))\cf14  \cf6 # OK\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo applies at most one implicit conversion to a variable. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  t\cf5 :\cf14  OverloadedTarget \cf5 =\cf14  Source\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf6 # Error: can't implicitly convert Source\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13                                    \cf6 # to Target\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Would fail because there's no direct conversion from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Source
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OverloadedTarget
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
For structs with a single field, you can generate an implicit constructor with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @fieldwise_init("implicit")
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init(\cf16 "implicit"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Counter\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  count\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  c\cf5 :\cf14  Counter \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 5\cf14   \cf6 # implicitly converts from Int\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Copy constructor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, a value can be copied either explicitly or implicitly:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Explicit copy\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  s \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 "Test string"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  s2 \cf5 =\cf14  s\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Implicit copy\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  i \cf5 =\cf14  \cf19 15\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  i2 \cf5 =\cf14  i\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To make a struct explicitly copyable, you need to:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
(Optionally) define a custom copy constructor if needed, written 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def __init__(out self, *, copy: Self)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 By adding the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait, Mojo can generate a default copy constructor for you unless you've written one yourself. This default method copies each field of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value into the new value. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait also defines a default 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method which provides an additional way to copy a value, versus calling the copy constructor directly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPet\cf5 (\cf14 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now this code works to make a copy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  mine \cf5 =\cf14  MyPet\cf5 (\cf16 "Loki"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  yours \cf5 =\cf14  mine\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 Technically, you could make a struct with a copy constructor and not add the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 Copyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  trait, but this is not recommended. Mojo would be able to copy the value, but you couldn't use the struct with any generic containers or functions that require the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 Copyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  trait.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The generated copy constructor simply copies each field from the existing value into the new value. For example, if you manually wrote the copy constructor for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it would look like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 *,\cf14  copy\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  copy\cf5 .\cf14 name\cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  copy\cf5 .\cf14 age\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This default copy constructor works in most cases, but there are a few cases where you need to define a custom copy constructor:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
One or more of the struct's fields is not 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The struct includes a non-owning type (like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 UnsafePointer}}), and you want to make a deep copy of the data.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The struct holds other resources (like file descriptors or network sockets) that need to be managed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Custom copy constructor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 What makes Mojo's copy behavior different, compared to other languages, is that copy constructor is designed to perform a deep copy of all fields in the type (as per {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/value-semantics/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 value semantics}}). That is, it copies heap-allocated values, rather than just copying the pointer.\
However, the Mojo compiler doesn't enforce this, so it's the type author's responsibility to implement copy constructor with value semantics.\
For example, here's a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HeapArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type with a custom copy constructor that performs a deep copy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 HeapArray\cf5 (\cf14 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  data\cf5 :\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ,\cf14  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  cap\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  val\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 =\cf14  size\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 cap \cf5 =\cf14  size \cf5 *\cf14  \cf19 2\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ](\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 cap\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 +\cf14  i\cf5 ).\cf14 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf14 val\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 *,\cf14  copy\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Deep-copy the existing value\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 =\cf14  copy\cf5 .\cf14 size\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 cap \cf5 =\cf14  copy\cf5 .\cf14 cap\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ](\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 cap\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 +\cf14  i\cf5 ).\cf14 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf14 copy\cf5 .\cf14 data\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # The lifetime of `copy` continues unchanged\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # We must free the heap-allocated data, but\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Mojo knows how to destroy the other fields\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 +\cf14  i\cf5 ).\cf14 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 data\cf5 .\cf14 free\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 append\cf5 (mut\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  val\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Update the array for demo purposes\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 if\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 <\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 cap\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 data \cf5 +\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ).\cf14 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf14 val\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             self\cf5 .\cf14 size \cf5 +=\cf14  \cf19 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 print(\cf16 "Out of bounds"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 dump\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Print the array contents for demo purposes\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf16 "["\cf5 ,\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf16 ""\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 if\cf14  i \cf5 >\cf14  \cf19 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13                 \cf5 print(\cf16 ", "\cf5 ,\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf16 ""\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 print(\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 data\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ],\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf16 ""\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf16 "]"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Notice that the copy constructor doesn't copy the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value (doing so would make the copied value refer to the same 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  memory address as the original value, which is a shallow copy). Instead, we initialize a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to allocate a new block of memory, and then copy over all the heap-allocated values (this is a deep copy).\
Thus, when we copy an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HeapArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , each copy has its own set of values on the heap, so changes to one array do not affect the other, as shown here:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 copies\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  a \cf5 =\cf14  HeapArray\cf5 (\cf19 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  b \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # Calls the copy method\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     a\cf5 .\cf14 dump\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # Prints [1, 1]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     b\cf5 .\cf14 dump\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # Prints [1, 1]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     b\cf5 .\cf14 append\cf5 (\cf19 2\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Changes the copied data\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     b\cf5 .\cf14 dump\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # Prints [1, 1, 2]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     a\cf5 .\cf14 dump\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # Prints [1, 1] (the original did not change)\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Two other things to note from the copy constructor:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (capital "S"). 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is an alias for the current type name (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HeapArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , in this example). Using this alias is a best practice to avoid any mistakes when referring to the current struct name.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is immutable because the default {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}} is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97this is a good thing because this function should not modify the contents of the value being copied.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 In 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 HeapArray
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 , we must use the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 __del__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  destructor to free the heap-allocated data when the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 HeapArray
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  lifetime ends, but Mojo automatically destroys all other fields when their respective lifetimes end. We'll discuss this destructor more in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Death of a value}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If your type doesn't use any pointers for heap-allocated data, then writing the constructor and copy constructor is all boilerplate code that you shouldn't have to write. For most structs that don't manage memory explicitly, you can just add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait to your struct definition, and Mojo will synthesize the copy constructor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 Mojo also calls upon the copy constructor when a value is passed to a function that takes the argument as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#transfer-arguments-var-and-"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb21 \ul var}} and when the lifetime of the given value does not end at that point. If the lifetime of the value does end there (usually indicated with the transfer sigil 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 ^
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 ), then Mojo instead invokes the move constructor.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Implicitly-copyable types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To make a type implicitly copyable, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 (\cf14 ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     pair \cf5 =\cf14  MyPair\cf5 (\cf19 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     copy \cf5 =\cf14  pair\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 pair\cf5 .\cf14 first\cf5 ,\cf14  copy\cf5 .\cf14 second\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait refines 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so adding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to your type gives you 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 's copy constructor and a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. It also gives you automatic 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conformance.\
The trait doesn't define any methods of its own: it serves as a signal to the compiler that it can insert calls to copy constructor as needed. For example, in the previous example, the compiler might generate code equivalent to:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 pair \cf5 =\cf14  MyPair\cf5 (\cf19 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 copy \cf5 =\cf14  pair\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A type should be implicitly copyable only if copying the type is inexpensive and has no side effects. Unnecessary copies can be a big drain on memory and performance, so use this trait with caution.\
In particular, any type that dynamically allocates memory or manages other resources probably shouldn't be implicitly copyable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Move constructor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Although copying values provides predictable behavior that matches Mojo's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/value-semantics/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 value semantics}}, copying some data types can be a significant hit on performance.\
Mojo uses the move constructor to transfer ownership of a value from one variable to another, without copying its fields. A value that's copyable but doesn't have a move constructor can still be transferred by making a copy and then discarding the original. So in this case, the move constructor serves as an optimization.\
To make a type movable:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait or conform to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
(Optionally) implement a custom move constructor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here's a movable (also copyable) version of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPet\cf5 (\cf14 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here's an example showing how to invoke the move constructor for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 moves\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  a \cf5 =\cf14  MyPet\cf5 (\cf16 "Bobo"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 2\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 a\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # Prints "bobo"\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  b \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 ^\cf14  \cf6 # the lifetime of `a` ends here\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 b\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # prints "bobo"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # print(a.name)  # ERROR: use of uninitialized value 'a'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you include the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait and don't define a move constructor, Mojo generates a default move constructor for you. This move constructor simply moves each of the fields to the new instance.\
The generated move constructor for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  would look like this if you wrote it yourself:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 *,\cf14  \cf5 deinit\cf14  take\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  take\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 ^\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  take\cf5 .\cf14 age\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The move constructor takes its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 take
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}}, which grants exclusive ownership of the value and marks it as destroyed at the end of the function. (For more information on the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/#field-lifetimes-during-destruct-and-move"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Field lifetimes during destruct and move}}).\
The move constructor uses the transfer sigil (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to indicate that ownership of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value transfers from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 take
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For trivial register-passable types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , omit the transfer sigil. Trivial types are always movable. Since they can't define custom move constructors or destructors, there's no special logic required to move.\
At the end of the move constructor method, Mojo immediately invalidates the original variable, preventing any access to it. Because the argument is declared as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it does not call the destructor, since that would destroy resources that have been transferred to the new instance. Invalidating the original variable is important to avoid memory errors on heap-allocated data, such as use-after-free and double-free errors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 A move constructor is not required to transfer ownership of a value. Mojo can copy the value and invalidate the original instance. You can learn more in the section about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#transfer-arguments-var-and-"}}{\fldrslt \ul ownership transfer}}.\cb1 \
\cb20 If copying a type is expensive, moving it with a move constructor is much more efficient. For example, if a type has heap-allocated data, a copy constructor typically needs to allocate new storage to make a deep copy of the data.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 For types without heap-allocated fields, you get no real benefit from the move constructor. Making copies of simple data types on the stack, like integers, floats, and booleans, is very cheap. Yet, if you allow your type to be copied, then there's generally no reason to disallow moves.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Custom move constructor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In practice, structs very rarely require a custom move constructor. A type might require a custom implementation if it has a pointer to itself or one of its fields, for example, since the struct's location in memory changes when it's moved.\
The move constructor performs a consuming move: it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#transfer-arguments-var-and-"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 transfers ownership}} of a value from one variable to another when the original variable's lifetime ends (also called a "destructive move").\
A critical feature of move constructors is that it takes the incoming value as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, meaning this method gets unique ownership of the value. Moreover, because this is a dunder method that Mojo calls only when performing a move (during ownership transfer), the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 take
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is guaranteed to be a mutable reference to the original value, not a copy (unlike other methods that may declare an argument as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but might receive the value as a copy if the method is called without the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#transfer-arguments-var-and-"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 ^
\fs32 \cb1  transfer sigil}}). That is, Mojo calls this move constructor only when the original variable's lifetime actually ends at the point of transfer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Move-only and immovable types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To ensure your type can't be implicitly copied, you can make it "move-only" by making it 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  but not 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . A move-only type can be passed to other variables and passed into functions with any argument convention (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 )\'97the only catch is that you must use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil to end the lifetime of a move-only type when assigning it to a new variable or when passing it as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/owned_pointer/OwnedPointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 OwnedPointer}} is an example of a move-only type: because it is designed to provide clear single ownership of a stored value, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be moved, but not copied.\
In some (rare) cases, you may not want a type to be copyable or movable. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/os/atomic/Atomic/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Atomic}} type is an example of a type that's neither copyable nor movable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb20 Trivial types\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 Trivial types like 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 Int
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 Bool
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 , or 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 Float64
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  are the most common types in systems programming. Mojo doesn't need special support for these. They have no ownership semantics, no destructors, and are always passed in CPU registers.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Trivial lifecycle methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The use of a decorator to identify trivial types will be phased out in favor of a more granular set of aliases, which are boolean flags set by the compiler:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait defines the alias 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __copy_ctor_is_trivial
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is an optimization hint that guarantees that the value can be copied by its bits from one location to another without causing any side effects.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait defines the alias 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __move_ctor_is_trivial
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is an optimization hint that guarantees that the value can be moved by its bits from one location to another without causing any side effects.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait defines the alias 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__is_trivial
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is a hint that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method is a no-op.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
